'''Francesca Solà i Verdú''' (born 14 March 1970) is a Balboan chemical engineer, entrepreneur, and the founder and Chairman of [[Grup Químic Central]] (GQC), one of the world's leading producers of {{w|per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances|PFAS}}, {{w|fluorochemicals}}, and {{w|fluoropolymers}}. She is widely regarded as a pioneer in high-purity fluorochemical production for the {{w|semiconductor}} industry, having built GQC from a small industrial fluoropolymer producer into a critical supplier to the semiconductor manufacturing supply chains of [[Balisca]] and [[Okaiken]]. At the same time, she has been labelled a "scientist of death" ({{lang|ca|científica de la mort}}) by environmental activists and community advocates for her role in the PFAS pollution scandals in [[Jala]] province, where GQC's production facilities have contaminated groundwater and drinking water supplies over more than a decade.

With an estimated net worth of $1.2 billion — derived from her 15% stake in GQC, which has a {{w|market capitalization}} of $8 billion — Solà is one of the richest people in [[Balboa]] and one of the wealthiest self-made women in the Abyalan region.

== Early life and education ==

Solà was born in [[Sant Cristòfor]] in 1970. She studied chemical engineering at the [[Universitat de Sant Cristòfor]], where she specialised in polymer chemistry and completed a thesis on fluoropolymer synthesis. After graduating in 1992, she worked briefly as a research assistant at a small industrial chemicals firm in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] before joining [[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB) in 1994.

== Career at Polímers de Balboa (1994–1997) ==

Solà was hired as a process engineer at PDB's Badia Curta facility in [[Jala]] province, where she was assigned to the company's fluoropolymer research group. Her work focused on the synthesis and processing of {{w|PTFE}} and related fluoropolymers for industrial applications. During this time, she became aware of the growing demand for high-purity fluorochemicals in the semiconductor industry, which was expanding rapidly in [[Balisca]] and [[Okaiken]].

Solà identified a gap in the domestic supply chain: semiconductor fabrication required a range of fluorine-based chemicals — including {{w|hydrofluoric acid}} for etching and cleaning, fluorine-based {{w|plasma etching}} gases, and {{w|PTFE}} components for {{w|ultrapure water}} systems — and no Balboan company was producing these chemicals at the purity levels required by semiconductor manufacturers. She proposed that PDB expand into high-purity fluorochemicals, but the company's management declined, considering the semiconductor chemicals market too small and specialised to justify the investment.

Solà left PDB in 1997 to pursue the opportunity independently.

== Founding Grup Químic Central (1998) ==

In 1998, Solà founded Grup Químic Central with backing from private investors and the Balboan government's industrial diversification programme. She established the company's first production facility in [[Jala]] province, near the [[Shendan]] coast, with access to {{w|fluorspar}} (calcium fluoride) from mining operations in the [[Yalabirga]] foothills and export logistics via the [[Balboa Canal]].

The company initially produced basic fluoropolymers — {{w|PTFE}}, {{w|PFA}}, and {{w|FEP}} — for industrial applications such as gaskets, seals, and linings for the chemical processing industry, as well as hydrofluoric acid for general industrial use. These products were sold primarily to the domestic market and to BTPMEU countries, with exports benefiting from the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]].

== Innovator ==

In the early 2000s, Solà recognised that GQC's existing fluorochemical expertise could be redirected toward the semiconductor market, which required far higher purity standards but commanded significantly higher margins. She led the company's investment in purification technology and quality control systems, and by the mid-2000s GQC had begun supplying high-purity etching agents and cleaning chemicals to semiconductor fabs in Balisca and Okaiken.

The pivot transformed GQC's economics. Semiconductor-grade fluorochemicals sold at a substantial premium to industrial-grade products, and the company's margins expanded rapidly. Solà took GQC public on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 2007, raising capital to expand its high-purity production capacity. She retained a 15% stake, making her a billionaire as the company's market capitalisation grew.

Under Solà's leadership, GQC expanded its product portfolio to include {{w|photoresist}} additives, {{w|anti-reflective coating}}s, and specialised fluoropolymer components for {{w|chemical delivery system}}s and {{w|wafer (electronics)|wafer}} handling equipment. By the 2010s, GQC had become a strategically important node in the global semiconductor supply chain, and its dominance in certain specialty fluorochemicals gave Balboa significant geopolitical leverage over the semiconductor supply chains of both Balisca and Okaiken — a position that has been compared to [[CIEA]]'s COBAL contract with Balisca.

Solà has been recognised as a pioneer in high-purity fluorochemical production, and her technical expertise in fluoropolymer chemistry and purification technology is widely acknowledged in the industry. She has been described as one of the most innovative entrepreneurs in Balboa's industrial sector, having identified and capitalised on a critical gap in the semiconductor supply chain that larger companies — including her former employer PDB — had overlooked.

== "Scientist of death": the PFAS scandals ==

Despite her achievements as an innovator, Solà's career has been overshadowed by the environmental consequences of GQC's operations. PFAS — the same chemicals that made GQC a critical supplier to the semiconductor industry — are highly persistent in the environment, often called "{{w|forever chemicals}}," and have been linked to contamination of water supplies, soil, and ecosystems, as well as to serious health problems including cancer, thyroid disease, and immune system disorders.

Under Solà's leadership, GQC has been the subject of three major environmental incidents in Jala province:

* '''2006 groundwater contamination''': Testing by the Jala provincial environmental authority detected elevated levels of PFAS in groundwater near GQC's production facility, traced to inadequate waste management practices. GQC was fined and ordered to upgrade its waste management systems.
* '''2013 repeated violations''': A follow-up investigation found that PFAS levels had increased despite the 2006 enforcement action, and that GQC's upgraded waste management system was not operating to design specifications. GQC was fined a second time and required to implement a comprehensive remediation plan.
* '''2019 drinking water contamination''': PFAS contamination was detected in the drinking water supplies of communities downstream from GQC's Jala facility, affecting approximately 12,000 residents. The incident prompted community protests and calls for the facility to be shut down. GQC was fined a third time and required to provide alternative water supplies.

Following the 2019 incident, environmental activists and community advocates in Jala began referring to Solà as a "scientist of death" ({{lang|ca|científica de la mort}}) — a label that has since been adopted by journalists and critics to describe the paradox at the heart of her career: a chemist who created chemicals that are both essential to modern technology and devastating to the environment and human health. The label captures the moral complexity of her work — the same scientific expertise that made her an innovator also made her a polluter, and the same chemicals that gave Balboa geopolitical leverage also poisoned the water of its own citizens.

Solà has been criticised for her response to the scandals. Critics argue that she downplayed the environmental and health risks of PFAS, emphasised the company's strategic importance to Balboa's economy and geopolitical position, and made only incremental improvements to GQC's waste management systems — improvements that proved insufficient, as demonstrated by the 2013 and 2019 incidents. Environmental groups have accused her of prioritising GQC's growth and profitability over the health of Jala's communities, and have called for her resignation and for criminal prosecution of GQC's management.

The Balboan government's reluctance to impose severe penalties or restrict GQC's production — because of the company's strategic importance to the semiconductor supply chains of Balisca and Okaiken — has further fuelled criticism of Solà, who critics say has exploited the company's geopolitical leverage to avoid meaningful accountability.

== Wealth and personal life ==

Solà's 15% stake in GQC, which has a {{w|market capitalization}} of approximately $8 billion, gives her an estimated net worth of $1.2 billion, making her one of the richest people in [[Balboa]] and one of the wealthiest self-made women in the Abyalan region. She lives in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and remains actively involved in GQC as Chairman, overseeing the company's strategic direction and its relationships with semiconductor manufacturers in Balisca and Okaiken.

She has been a controversial figure in Balboan public life, admired by some as a pioneering entrepreneur who built a strategically important company from scratch, and reviled by others as a polluter whose chemicals have caused lasting environmental and public health damage. The Balboan business press has described her as "the most admired and most despised woman in Balboa."

== Legacy ==

Solà's legacy is defined by the central paradox of her career: she is simultaneously an innovator and a polluter, a pioneer and a "scientist of death." Her achievements as an entrepreneur and chemical engineer are undeniable — she identified a critical gap in the semiconductor supply chain, built a company to fill it, and created a strategically important asset for Balboa — but they are inseparable from the environmental and public health damage caused by GQC's PFAS pollution in Jala.

Her career reflects the broader tensions of the Balboan economic model, which prioritises industrial growth, strategic leverage, and export competitiveness — often at the expense of environmental protection and the health of communities in the industrial provinces. The government's reluctance to hold GQC accountable for its pollution, because of the company's strategic importance, is part of a broader pattern in which Balboa's geopolitical and economic interests take precedence over environmental and public health concerns.

The "scientist of death" label, whatever its merits, captures a question that Solà's career poses but does not answer: whether innovation that comes at the cost of environmental and human damage can be justified by its strategic and economic benefits — and who should bear the cost of that damage.

